00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:32.360
So each customer's AD
runs in a special project

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.789
on a segregated network
that's dedicated just for you.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:38.240
So you're not sharing that with
any of the other customers.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.929
We also bake into our
service the principles

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:42.740
of AD delegation.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.390
So we have a preconfigured
admin account for you to use.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:49.090
And you're welcome to create
additional users and groups

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.700
to use with your Active
Directory tiered administration

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.890
models as you see fit.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:58.950
Also, it works alongside with
your existing on-premises AD.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.780
Based on your security
posture and needs,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.840
we support, both one-way and
two-way forest level trust

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.340
with your existing
Active Directory

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.350
domains, one or more domains.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.840
So whatever works well for you.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.570
What the service is really doing
is going ahead and automating

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.627
a bunch of different
best practices, which

00:09:15.627 --> 00:09:17.710
you would otherwise have
to worry about and manage

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.260
on your own into the
nature of our design.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.570
For instance, when domain
controllers come up,

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.580
they come up one in
each zone, so it's

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:28.500
highly available in
whatever region you specify.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.830
They're running a bunch
of diagnostics regularly

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:33.010
every few minutes
and making sure

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.510
that the Active Directory domain
health is getting populated

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.280
and reported back.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.470
We're backing up your
Active Directory domain data

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.830
every single day so
that we could recover

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.490
in the instance of any issues.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.820
But addressing
the IAM challenges

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:49.400
we mentioned earlier and
the operational challenges

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.490
by having engineers or
bill skilled in both Active

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.860
Directory and Google Cloud
as you build out the service.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.860
And the security aspect
is so clearly ingrained

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.050
into the service
that if you just

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:04.600
ask us to make an
AD domain available,

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.790
we don't just go ahead and
make it available on any

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:07.660
of our networks.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:10.120
You authorize
specific VPC networks,

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.582
and the AD domain is
accessible only from those.

00:10:12.582 --> 00:10:14.290
So if you're in a
segregated environment,

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:15.415
you can definitely do that.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.840
Building out and talking
more about the security

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:24.390
aspects of the service, we're
automating our firewall rules.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:27.198
And one of the challenges that
happens when you self-deploy

00:10:27.198 --> 00:10:29.240
is you may have the right
firewall rules in place

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.570
on day one.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.390
But somebody else
came in after the fact

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:33.920
and changed the firewall
rules, because they

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.087
were deploying a web server.

00:10:35.087 --> 00:10:37.190
And before you know it,
you have sensitive data

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.860
coming onto the same network.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:41.280
So because it runs in
an isolated network,

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.310
and we automate and
enforce the firewall rules,

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:46.430
we're able to address
this very well.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.310
As you're aware, Windows
Server releases typically

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:50.800
monthly security patches.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.545
So we go ahead and patch
our domain controllers.

00:10:53.545 --> 00:10:54.920
Now, when you're
updating AD, you

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.140
need to make sure that you're
doing it systematically

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.180
so the AD domain
remains available

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.095
while the OS is being updated.

00:11:01.095 --> 00:11:03.470
So we go ahead and take care
of all of that orchestration

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:06.510
on your behalf.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.260
The service level APIs
operations are integrated

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:10.800
with Cloud Audit logging.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.820
So if somebody came in and
reset the admin account password

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.710
to our service API,
you would definitely

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:20.190
be able to see that in Cloud
Audit Logging from the get-go.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.440
The last two points are
pretty interesting and key

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:26.260
to understanding how the
service works with security.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.560
Anytime our service
code needs access

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.930
to sensitive Active
Directory credentials,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.670
we're taking the utmost
care to make sure

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:35.980
that we're protecting
them in a safe manner.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:39.540
We actually have a key for
Active Directory domain,

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:43.590
but Active Directory managed
domain held in Cloud KMS.

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:47.220
And this key is what is utilized
to go ahead and make sure

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.980
that your secrets are
encrypted and your AD domain

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:55.160
specific credentials are
getting rotated every 30 days.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:57.800
We also enforce safeguards
for preventing elevation

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.330
to Domain Admin.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:02.110
In particular, a lot of
our service level code

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.160
has actually been
through and released

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.410
after it passes peer reviews and
our internal security reviews.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:12.520
In the event that a human needs
to go in and access or manage

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.740
the domain in the
event of an incident,

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.270
we actually have a whole
internal process in place.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:18.270
They need to be on-call.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:19.770
They need to be in
the right groups.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.190
And they get that
elevated access

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.170
for only very short
amounts of time

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:24.920
that may be needed
to address the issue.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.381
And then it goes back to being
fully automated from then on.

00:12:28.381 --> 00:12:30.900
So I've been talking about
this service for a few minutes.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.490
You're probably interested in
taking a look under the hood.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:34.280
So let's jump in.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.200
So we have the UI for
the managed service.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.170
As you can see, I have
precreated two managed AD

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.610
domains here for you--

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.860
corporate and partners.

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.700
If you go in and
dig a little deeper,

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.100
you'll see that it is running--

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.920
the domain controllers
are deployed in us-west1.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.940
The Active Directory
is working just fine.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.090
Let's jump in.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.880
You'll see a few more details
come up about the service.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.920
In particular, I wanted to focus
your attention on the networks.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.270
So you can see, though I have
other networks in this project,

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.740
it's only available on
the authorized network.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.315
This one.

00:13:25.315 --> 00:13:27.190
And there's a few more
aspects of the service

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.270
you can read through.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:34.010
Now I want to pause here and
ask you, how are you guys doing?

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:35.250
Oh, good?

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:36.270
Head nods and thumbs up.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.100
One sign.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.760
As you think about what
it goes into making sure

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.600
an Active Directory
environment is up and healthy.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:44.310
Is DNS up?

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.410
Is the application working?

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:47.630
Is LDAP working?

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:48.740
Is Kerberos working?

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:50.570
Are people able
to connect to it?

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.190
Are the right
signals being sent?

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:52.910
I could go on.

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:55.520
It's tens of items
that are checked--

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.770
on each and every
domain controller,

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:01.020
an aggregated backup to give
you that simple thumbs up.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.613
And that's what's going into
that health monitoring signal.

00:14:03.613 --> 00:14:05.280
So we're taking care
of that complexity,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.580
so you can just focus on
the service being available

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:09.260
and be productive with it.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:13.220
And I've actually integrated
the signal with Stackdriver.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:15.210
So as I go ahead
and dig in, you'll

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:18.330
see our Stackdriver UI
come up in a second.

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.150
And what you're noticing is
that the moment it comes up,

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:23.610
you're able to get a simple
graph of how the service has

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:24.703
been performing.

00:14:24.703 --> 00:14:26.370
So you can see, as
we expect, there were

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:27.990
no issues with the service.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.560
It's all good.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:31.690
Using the Stackdriver
UI capabilities,

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:33.490
you can very quickly
go and take a look

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:37.780
how did it perform over six
hours, over the last day,

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:38.820
over the last week.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:40.720
And you can notice this
managed AD domain has

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:43.360
been behaving perfectly fine.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:45.030
You can actually
get at-a-glance view

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.790
of not just this domain but all
your Managed Active Directory

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.150
domains.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:51.845
So if I drop the
filter on the FQDN,

00:14:51.845 --> 00:14:53.220
you'll see both
the rows come up.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.290
So both the corporate
and the partner's domain

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.310
have been behaving well
over the entire week.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:59.190
So this saves you time
in being able to get

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:00.715
a quick, at-a-glance view.

00:15:00.715 --> 00:15:03.090
And if you ever needed to go
back and investigate things,

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:06.583
you can see how it
performed historically.

00:15:06.583 --> 00:15:08.000
What we have done
with the service

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.920
is really distill
down all the items

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.580
that go into creating it
to a few simple steps.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:17.240
How many of you
remember DCPromo?

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:17.898
Yes?

00:15:17.898 --> 00:15:19.690
You've seen how it's
evolved over the years

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.910
from it being a very
specific bunch of inputs that

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:25.910
need to be given to somewhat
more simplified but still

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.730
multiple-step process
in the Add Rules Wizard

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:30.208
for Windows Server.

00:15:30.208 --> 00:15:32.750
And what we do is we go ahead
and take all of that complexity

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:35.390
down to what's the absolute
minimum set of things

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:37.130
we need from you.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:38.490
And I have to precreate it.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:40.370
I'll try and zoom into it.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.440
All we need is the name
of your Active Directory

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:46.242
domain, the network you
wanted to be authorized on.

00:15:46.242 --> 00:15:48.200
So it could be default.
You could select others

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:51.858
in the project, the IP
ranges we should use,

00:15:51.858 --> 00:15:53.650
and which region would
you like to bring up

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:55.660
the domain controllers in.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:58.740
Another interesting thing I
should call out is in GCP,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.560
our VPC networks are
multi-region aware.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.230
So if I make it available
on the default network

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.300
and the default network
has subnets from 5,

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.450
6, 10 different regions, then
your infrastructure running

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:12.240
and all of those regions can
still access this Managed AD

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.060
Domain even when the domain
controllers are being deployed

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:17.520
in the us-west2, so something
to think about as you design

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:21.000
your infrastructure on GCP.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.890
So this is a service-level view.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.640
I also wanted to hit
one more point, which

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:27.180
is you can utilize your
standard Active Directory

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:31.180
tooling to manage this Active
Directory Managed Service.

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:33.752
So let's jump over.

00:16:33.752 --> 00:16:37.920
I'm already RDPed
in to a Windows VM.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:41.430
Now this VM is domain join
to the GCP-managed Active

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:42.900
Directory domain.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.450
You can see I'm logged in
via a user account that

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.420
sits inside the corporate
domain I showed you

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:48.420
a couple of minutes ago.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:54.910
You can now see that I'm
looking at the service

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.400
through the Active Directory
Users and Computers UI,

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:00.010
which I'm guessing a lot of
you are probably familiar with.

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.680
The same VM we are now
RDPed into, as expected,

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.540
has a computer account in
the Active Directory domain.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:09.400
So you could use AD you
see to do other items.

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:11.230
Those are a few who
are using the newer

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:13.569
tool, which is the Active
Directory Administrative

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:14.230
Center.

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:16.770
That works well with this too.

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.569
You can see that I've
organized my users

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.250
and drops into two OUs.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.980
One for finance, the
other for marketing.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:26.069
And instead of finance,
I have a few users,

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:29.810
I have a couple of groups
precreated and so on.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:31.750
Also, Active
Directory operations

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.130
are scripted a lot of the time.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:36.370
So this also works with
Active Directory PowerShell.

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:40.180
This Is the standard get AD
group member, Active Directory

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:41.110
PowerShell Command.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.850
And as I go ahead
and run it, you

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:44.980
will see that it'll
show two of those users

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:46.920
are a part of that group.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:48.780
So whichever may be
the standard ED tool

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.700
that you're used to
utilizing, you actually

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.920
use that on the
infrastructure VM

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.650
that you control to connect
up to this managed AD service.

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.750
So that's the service
that's been under the hood.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:02.100
Let's come back to the slides.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:05.400
And I'm especially excited
to invite on stage Kenny

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:08.010
Hill from Capital One,
who will come and share

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.930
Capital One's
experiences running

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.460
Active Directory on GCP.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:12.120
Welcome, Kenny.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.162
Excited to have you here.

00:18:16.885 --> 00:18:17.760
KENNY HILL: Hi, guys.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.680
I'm Kenny Hill from Capital One.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:22.710
I'm in the identity and
access management space.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.010
My main focus is directories
with an emphasis on the cloud.

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:32.752
Thank you, Sid.

00:18:32.752 --> 00:18:35.630
At Capital One,
we are still very

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:37.610
dependent on Active Directory.

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:41.920
As I imagine, many of
you are here today.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:45.600
Many of you have probably gone
through mergers, acquisitions,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.860
maybe a handful of
domain migrations.

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:52.700
Your directories are
possibly fairly complex.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.470
You have a laundry list
of applications that are

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:58.410
dependent on Active Directory.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.690
Fast forward a bit to
taking your directory

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:05.400
into the cloud where you have
a whole new level of capacity

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:10.440
management, a new
type of monitoring

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.250
and really does the
same security model

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:19.920
that you had on-premise hold
true to being in the cloud.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:23.370
We currently domain join all
windows in most of our Linux

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:26.230
to Active Directory
during the boot process.

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:29.400
So this is built
into our image where

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:33.600
before that any instance is even
available, it's domain joined.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:38.280
Doing this allows us to apply
default domain policies, fully

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.310
control who's able
to authenticate,

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.695
including local
administrative rights.

00:19:43.695 --> 00:19:46.470
And these many commercial
off-the-shelf products

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:48.120
are just dependent
on Active Directory.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:51.600
Examples are anything with
IIS integration or SQL

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.700
dependencies.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:57.360
When we first got started, we
domain joined our instances

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:59.410
to our on-premise directory.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:00.070
This worked.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:04.470
It was also not the most popular
due to the number of firewall

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:05.370
ports we had opened.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:10.380
So think about opening
every application project,

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.860
I don't know how many have
you gone to a network review

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:17.700
and said, I need some AD ports.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.710
Well, no, I don't
want all of them.

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:27.760
I only need like
44,000 of the 64,000.

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.720
We also experienced a little
bit of latency on our domain

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:35.340
joins with this and a little bit
slower log on policy processing

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:36.420
time.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.330
One thing to point
out in this model

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:43.170
is it also kind of pins you
to a single point of failure

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:44.110
with your network.

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:47.610
So if you were to lose
your network connectivity,

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:51.930
you are not really taking full
advantage of built-in Active

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:54.340
Directory resiliency.

00:20:54.340 --> 00:21:01.290
So AD next closest site
gives you a little bit more

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:03.210
with DC locator
where it can find

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:06.225
where you want it to go next.

00:21:06.225 --> 00:21:08.350
SIDDHARTH BHAI: That's
actually really interesting.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.788
And I wanted to reflect on
that because sometimes when

00:21:10.788 --> 00:21:13.330
customers start out, you first
think about just connecting up

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:16.180
your networks through our
hybrid networking solution.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:18.420
But as Kenny's team started
experimenting with that,

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:21.135
you notice that it was
leading to a higher latency.

00:21:21.135 --> 00:21:23.260
And so then right upon you,
Kenny, do share with us

00:21:23.260 --> 00:21:25.330
what did you do next to
lower that latency down?

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:27.850
KENNY HILL: Sure, Sid.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:32.200
We knew that you'd lie going
back to on-premise presented

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:32.830
too much risk.

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:36.220
If we were going to require
everyone to domain join

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:39.317
and we lost network, then
applications couldn't scale.

00:21:39.317 --> 00:21:40.900
And that's part of
going to the cloud.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:43.600
You want to be able
to scale up and down.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:49.000
So we decided to expand our
enterprise directory to run

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.930
on GCP compute instances.

00:21:52.930 --> 00:22:00.220
Essentially, we're treating
GCP as infrastructure

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:03.280
as a service for our
Active Directory.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:07.180
This model worked, and it
gives us more resiliency

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:08.305
that our business required.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:16.510
In order to make
AD truly resilient,

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:20.050
though, in this model, you may
not achieve the cost savings

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:24.960
that you're being asked to get
to with moving to the cloud.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.250
You're going to have to run
a large number of domain

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:27.790
controllers.

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:29.373
You're going to run
in multiple zones.

00:22:29.373 --> 00:22:33.880
You're going to run in
multiple regions where you are.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:38.290
You also are going to take
on a whole new level--

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:42.730
and I mentioned it--
of capacity management.

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.050
There's a new type
of monitoring.

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:51.270
And there's a lot of
automation building out

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:56.810
domain controllers,
monitoring site reliability,

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.800
to keep domain
controllers running

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.390
as infrastructure as a service.

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:08.965
One of the challenges
for us was staffing.

00:23:08.965 --> 00:23:11.400
Active Directory he's
been around 20 years.

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:14.190
I don't know how many have you
been utilizing for 20 years.

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:19.410
To take a traditional
team of Active Directory

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:24.090
subject matter experts
and have them also--

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:27.030
still remain really
good at keeping

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:30.480
Active Directory
running and secure,

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:35.400
but also learn new
CI/CD, site reliability,

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:41.160
and other dev strategies
is a bit of a challenge.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:43.730
There's a large potential
gain in this model,

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:46.970
but it's something
you want to weigh

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.343
against the additional
overhead and challenges.

00:23:50.343 --> 00:23:51.260
SIDDHARTH BHAI: Right.

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:53.540
So the interesting thing
from these experiences

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:56.240
is you saw Capital One
deploy self-managed

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:58.370
Active Directory in
production, which is definitely

00:23:58.370 --> 00:23:59.720
a supported choice.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:01.250
And our similar
talk last year, we

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:05.040
went into detail how you can
set that up in best practices.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.150
However, oftentimes as you
look to do that, you end up

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.100
realizing that there
is more operational

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.560
overhead your teams
are having to take on.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:14.090
What we'll hear
from Kenny is how

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:16.070
is it that Capital
One looked to reduce

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:20.693
their operational overhead by
utilizing Google solutions?

00:24:20.693 --> 00:24:22.610
KENNY HILL: I'm pretty
sure these are probably

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:24.820
Sid's favorite slides.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:29.420
Managed directories in
the cloud is the direction

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:32.360
Capital One is trying to go.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:37.070
We have not quite figured
out what sits in the central

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:40.250
as our central repository
for authentication

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:41.780
and authorization.

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:43.970
What we do know,
though, is we are

00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:46.580
going to still have
Active Directory dependent

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:50.840
applications for any
foreseeable future.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:54.320
Utilizing managed AD as a
software-as-a-service model

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:59.540
allows us to reduce our
normal day-to-day domain admin

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:03.350
directory and
infrastructure tasks,

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:08.640
and focus more on additional
identity access management

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:09.640
components in the cloud.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:18.590
Removing the computer
traffic directly

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:21.080
to our enterprise
directory also allowed

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:24.350
us to reduce some of the
number of domain controllers

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:27.630
that were deploying
in the cloud.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:30.520
So also adds a little bit--
a few security benefits

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:33.023
that we'll go into in a bit.

00:25:33.023 --> 00:25:34.190
SIDDHARTH BHAI: Makes sense.

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:36.010
And so what you hear
from Capital One

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:40.040
is how they then started using
the GCP-managed AD domain.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:42.870
However, this is a standalone
to me and in its own forest.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:44.620
So, Kenny, can you
share how you connected

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:47.350
this with your existing
Active Directory topology?

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:48.700
KENNY HILL: Sure.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:51.940
What we did as you
can see is it--

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:56.830
It's a huge requirement for
us to continue to manage

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.070
in our own identities.

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:01.720
So we are a
multi-cloud, and we want

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:07.750
to treat our enterprise
directory as a hub for really

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:09.920
any service we're going to use.

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:14.830
You can see in this model, we
are creating a one-way forest

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:21.410
trust from managed AD to
our enterprise directory.

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:27.130
This allows us to
continue to utilize

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:34.880
our corporate credentials for
authentication or AD groups

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.650
that we have built
into our provisioning

00:26:36.650 --> 00:26:40.630
system for authorization.

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:43.960
We already had domain
controllers running in GCP.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:48.490
So we kept those in place,
and we just reduced the number

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:52.108
that we had deployed in this.

00:26:52.108 --> 00:26:57.550
This gives us more or less
dependency on the network,

00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:59.040
going back to on-premise.

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.720
So almost everything
in Active Directory

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.065
will work with an isolated
domain controller.

00:27:04.065 --> 00:27:05.440
There are a couple
of things that

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:07.648
won't work like password
reset, but hopefully, you're

00:27:07.648 --> 00:27:14.050
not using a cloud for
password resets like that.

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:18.730
We domain join compute
instance to managed AD.

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:22.980
Then all user authentication
traffic or authorization

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:25.570
with your AD groups is
going to go from the managed

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:30.030
AD to enterprise directory,
while all computer

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:34.060
authentication traffic
is going to go to--

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:38.003
And even GPO stuff is going
to go to the managed AD.

00:27:38.003 --> 00:27:39.170
SIDDHARTH BHAI: Interesting.

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:41.090
So we can see now
how Capital One

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:44.270
was able to use the managed
AD domain with the trust

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.730
relationship with their
existing on-premises AD

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:48.620
and get operations to work.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:50.240
What's especially
exciting is, Kenny,

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:52.670
if you can share how this
actually held an answer cloud

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.160
security overall.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:56.530
KENNY HILL: Definitely.

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:59.845
So let me go over a couple
of the security benefits.

00:27:59.845 --> 00:28:00.970
This slide is kind of busy.

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:09.760
You guys in the room
may not be scared to put

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.710
your infrastructure
into a public cloud,

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:15.490
but I guarantee you,
there are a few naysayers.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:19.040
There are few people in
your company who are scared.

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:21.080
And it's really fair, right?

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:27.730
The number of breaches we read
about on almost a weekly basis,

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:34.030
and Active Directory is
oftentimes maybe not called

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:37.510
out in the news article, but
in the post-follow-up of how

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:38.710
the breach occurred.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.900
Active Directory was one of the
things that was compromised.

00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:46.340
So in this model, the
top the top diagram,

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:49.420
you can see that a
computer directly domain

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:52.390
join to the corporate forest.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:54.460
You may not be
aware, but a computer

00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:57.010
can authenticate
the same as a user.

00:28:57.010 --> 00:29:02.110
If you're familiar with the
Active Directory schema,

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:08.050
really a computer is a member
of the user object class.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:11.230
In the top diagram,
a computer domain

00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:13.840
joined has the
capability of running

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:16.900
a system if you were
to lose that instance

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:18.370
to query your AD data.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:21.400
And no, you're most
likely not going

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:24.460
to have sensitive data
in your directory,

00:29:24.460 --> 00:29:28.030
though it's not
really a good idea

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.240
to really give somebody
a path on how to--

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:34.470
of your user accounts
or your computer names.

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:38.100
In the bottom model, utilizing
a resource forest model,

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:41.980
the computer has no ability
to query the directory

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:46.380
without additional credentials.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:50.190
This next slide's a little
bit more about monitoring.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:55.260
Active Directory is super
chatty, so logs often roll in--

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:57.270
when you're running
in the cloud and you

00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:03.930
have a large number of instances
of spinning up or scaling,

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:09.000
if you're not really
careful, it is easy for--

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:14.160
to hit in Windows event log
limits per second, where

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:17.610
you aren't getting the logs.

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:20.010
I'm not sure if any
of you have seen that,

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:22.220
but there's times that
things are occurring,

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:23.970
and they're not going
into the event logs,

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:26.550
because you're going
over your limit.

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:31.950
So in the bottom model, removing
the computer authentication,

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:36.900
GPO processing, and
all of that, you're

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:40.120
able to focus on your
user authentication.

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:43.080
And this allows you to be
a little bit more precise

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:45.600
and look for
promiscuous activity.

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:49.000
You can still monitor the
traffic to the managed AD.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:52.650
It's just not as critical
as monitoring the user

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:53.914
authentications.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:31:00.510
Many of you may be familiar
with selective authentication.

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:03.870
If not, setting up selective
authentication on the trust

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:09.060
allows you to
really choose who's

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:12.240
going to authenticate
across the trust.

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:18.780
When you're fully mature
in the cloud, really,

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:23.490
you shouldn't have
users logging in.

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:27.250
With that said, that is a
very long transformation,

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:30.200
and there will
always be exceptions.

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:34.080
So you should only need
to have service or system

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:37.230
accounts, whichever
term you use,

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:40.510
that are authenticating
on your systems.

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:44.490
And then maybe a small
subset of privileged users

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:50.850
who are allowed to directly log
in to an instance in the cloud

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.610
if there is SEV
incident or something.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:06.250
So with that smaller set of
credentials that you're are

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:12.220
allowing, you can then apply
a more complex password set,

00:32:12.220 --> 00:32:16.810
and you can rotate the
passwords more frequently.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:20.290
You can deny most regular
users from ever logging

00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:22.570
into anything.

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:25.090
Say if you're running Citrix
or something like that,

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:30.520
you can build out an OU
in managed AD that has

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:33.070
allowed to authenticate on it.

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:36.080
And those users could log
into the Citrix instances,

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:40.160
but they would never be able
to log into anything else.

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:43.730
In the corp model above at
the top, what I'm showing is

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:47.540
this is completely dependent on
a local resource access control

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:48.290
list.

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:51.640
And anyone with administrative
rights can add more.

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:53.810
So maybe they want to
add their co-worker,

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:56.000
because I think
they need access.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:00.080
In the bottom model with a
selective authentication,

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:02.480
really, you're
adding another layer.

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:05.780
So you have the local
access control list,

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.450
but then you also
have another layer

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:10.550
that you can tie into
your provisioning system,

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:12.040
and you're controlling
who's going

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:14.780
to ever be able to go
across the forest trust.

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:19.460
So, essentially, you're
doubling your access checks.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:26.050
To recap, utilizing
GCP-managed AD

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:28.890
as a resource forest
allows us to maintain

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:33.390
the resiliency in our
business requires,

00:33:33.390 --> 00:33:36.900
reduces our
operational overhead,

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.520
and continue to utilize
our internal directories

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:46.530
for authentication and
authorization, while also

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:50.830
adding a few additional
security elements to us.

00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:52.680
So we're looking
forward to consuming

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:54.780
in general availability.

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:57.060
And, Sid, I want to thank
you and your whole team

00:33:57.060 --> 00:34:00.900
for allowing us to take part in
the alpha and inviting us back.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:02.430
And, everybody,
thank you very much,

00:34:02.430 --> 00:34:04.980
and feel free to
connect afterwards.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:06.660
I love questions.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:07.410
Thanks, Sid.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:09.118
SIDDHARTH BHAI: Thank
you so much, Kenny.

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:16.230
So to recap where we are at,
you've heard about our service,

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:19.170
you've heard how Capital One has
been using it and connecting it

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.028
with their existing domains.

00:34:21.028 --> 00:34:22.820
And what I wanted to
do in the next segment

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:26.603
is speed through a few
specific uses of the service.

00:34:26.603 --> 00:34:29.020
So as you're mulling about,
well, how do I use this thing?

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:30.730
Which use cases are interesting?

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:33.230
And I'm going to go
through five GCP use case

00:34:33.230 --> 00:34:35.550
very quickly to help
spark some ideas.

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:37.310
So let's jump in.

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:39.110
First up, that's
probably the first thing

00:34:39.110 --> 00:34:40.360
that you do with
Active Directory

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:42.110
when you're bringing
up an infrastructure.

00:34:42.110 --> 00:34:43.730
You go ahead and domain join it.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:46.120
And so we have made investments
to make that domain join

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:47.960
process be easier.

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:49.130
How so?

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:51.920
Remember when we created
an Active Directory domain,

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.489
you asked for it to be available
on an authorized network?

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:56.989
And, by the way, you can come
back and update those networks

00:34:56.989 --> 00:34:58.310
later as your needs change.

00:34:58.310 --> 00:34:59.630
No problems.

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:03.140
What we do is we actually
create a private-managed DNS

00:35:03.140 --> 00:35:06.920
zone in each of those networks
so that when you then bring up

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:11.100
a VM, it can just discover the
managed AD domain without you

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.790
having to do any kind
of complicated per-VM

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:14.990
configuration.

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:17.220
So that's how domain join works.

00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:20.930
You also saw Capital One talk
about how they are actually

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:23.660
solving another interesting
identity requirement I hear

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:26.000
often from our
customers, which is,

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:30.025
I want my user identity to
still originate on-premises.

00:35:30.025 --> 00:35:31.400
And so what you
can do is you can

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:35.840
have your VM be domain joined
to GCP-managed Active Directory

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:40.250
domain while your users can
still reside on-premises.

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:43.630
Let's jump in and take a
look at this in a demo.

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:45.712
In the interest of time,
I prerecorded the video,

00:35:45.712 --> 00:35:47.920
and I'm just going to talk
through it as it comes up.

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:52.760
Great.

00:35:52.760 --> 00:35:54.810
So what you see as we
go ahead and hit start

00:35:54.810 --> 00:36:01.118
on the video is when
this starts playing--

00:36:01.118 --> 00:36:03.160
can we do that from the
back of the room, please?

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:03.420
Great.

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:04.150
Thank you.

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:05.710
So as this comes
up, you will see

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:08.830
that I've already created
a managed Active Directory

00:36:08.830 --> 00:36:09.820
domain.

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:11.740
And that managed
Active Directory domain

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:13.625
is ad.demo.com.

00:36:13.625 --> 00:36:15.000
So we have the
domain controllers

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:19.180
deployed in us-central1,
and it's in a healthy state.

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:21.370
As I jump in, you
can actually see

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:23.770
that I've preconfigured this
managed Active Directory

00:36:23.770 --> 00:36:26.800
domain with a one-way
forest level trust,

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:28.480
going back to your
on-premises AD

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.940
domain, which is onpremad.demo.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:34.040
So that's the name of
my on-premises domain.

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:38.190
I'm now going to go ahead and
switch out into an application.

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:42.890
So you're actually seeing
SharePoint run in a VM on GCE.

00:36:42.890 --> 00:36:46.160
And the user identity, which
you have used to access it just

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:49.630
now, was actually a
user account to start

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:51.770
in your on-premises domain.

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:53.810
So this is how you can
go ahead and actually

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:57.110
have your Active Directory
domain, the managed one,

00:36:57.110 --> 00:37:00.140
help your infrastructure while
your identities originate

00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:01.227
on premises.

00:37:01.227 --> 00:37:02.810
Let's come back to
the slides and dive

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:03.770
into the next use case.

00:37:06.950 --> 00:37:08.420
And so as that
comes up, the thing

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:13.613
that you think about doing after
domain join is group policy.

00:37:13.613 --> 00:37:15.030
So we're going to
go ahead and see

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:17.820
how we can have a group
policy be working well

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:20.900
with the GCP-managed AD domain.

00:37:20.900 --> 00:37:24.020
What you can see here is inside
of the GCP-managed Active

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:26.570
Directory domain, I have an OU.

00:37:26.570 --> 00:37:28.820
And as your computer
accounts come up and domain

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:30.980
join the GCP-managed
AD domain, you

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:32.780
have those computer
accounts coming up

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:35.260
in that organizational unit.

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:38.590
We can actually go
out and create a GPO

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:40.840
that links directly to that OU.

00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:43.750
And what this enables for you
is your computer accounts,

00:37:43.750 --> 00:37:45.610
your service account's
related group policy.

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:48.250
All of that is processed
entirely in the cloud

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:50.800
without having to go back
to on-premises improving

00:37:50.800 --> 00:37:54.240
performance and latency for you.

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:56.005
Next use case, number three.

00:37:56.005 --> 00:37:57.630
We actually have an
interesting feature

00:37:57.630 --> 00:37:59.820
in GCP called as OS Login.

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:01.890
Have some of you heard about it?

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:02.860
A few.

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:04.950
One of the things that
we hear about on OS Login

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.600
is it works great for Linux.

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:09.210
How can I get something
similar for Windows?

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.180
So using GCP-managed Active
Directory domain, what

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:15.000
you can do is you can actually
have the same group policy

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.520
processing mechanism,
we utilized

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:23.760
to let you be able to control
RDP access onto your VMs.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:27.390
In particular, we actually
precreate for you a RDP admins

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:30.240
group, and you can just add and
remove people from that group.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:32.310
And that sort of users
are the only ones

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:35.650
who will then be able to
RDP to those windows VMs.

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:37.650
I do want to call out
that while the service can

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:40.440
enable this, all the
things we spoke about IAM--

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.660
because this is not your
infrastructure-- do apply.

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:44.370
So you still want
to think about who

00:38:44.370 --> 00:38:46.500
has compute instance
admin, set IAM

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:49.860
policy, and other permissions
on those GCP projects

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:51.110
while you utilize the service.

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:54.510
But this is a use
case we also enable.

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:57.090
So far, we've been covering a
lot of traditional enterprise

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:57.820
use cases.

00:38:57.820 --> 00:38:59.890
So let's mix things up a bit.

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:02.710
I'm now going to talk about
managed instance groups.

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:04.710
Now this is actually
an interesting feature

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:06.870
that GCP already
supports, that allows

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:10.770
you to treat a large collection
of VMs as a single entity

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:13.222
to control and manage.

00:39:13.222 --> 00:39:15.180
One of the interesting
things that we have done

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:18.060
is we have a script and a
solutions guide which we intend

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:21.240
to publish with the beta of the
service, which actually allows

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:24.840
you to have these VMs coming up
in the managed instance group.

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:29.140
And we automatically join to
the GCP-managed AD domain.

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:30.850
Now what this does
is it, therefore,

00:39:30.850 --> 00:39:33.160
allows you to utilize
managed instance

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.930
groups in interesting ways.

00:39:35.930 --> 00:39:41.260
For instance, IS Servers tend
to be an interesting web server

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:43.540
that a lot of you
still potentially use.

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:45.950
And ISF servers and
other websites contents

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:48.680
are good target use cases
for managed instance scripts.

00:39:51.430 --> 00:39:54.220
Let's go one step further and
actually cover a fifth use

00:39:54.220 --> 00:39:57.370
case, which is how can this
managed AD service actually

00:39:57.370 --> 00:39:59.690
help modernize
your applications.

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:02.110
So for that, we're going to
go ahead and switch to a demo.

